  "worked_solution": "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since \n\n$$\nQ(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\n So,  it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ only.\"",
